WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.061 --> 00:00:02.190
(light music)

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.960
- When I look at a map, I see opportunity.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.490
- [Narrator] This is Patrick Quayle.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.857
He's a global network planning executive

00:00:06.857 --> 00:00:07.690
(Patrick speaking
indistinctly on background)

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:08.640
at United Airlines

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.490
which means he decides
where the airline flies,

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.280
when it flies, and what
type of aircraft it flies.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.870
- I can talk about planes forever.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.330
- [Narrator] Quayle has more
than 15 years of experience

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.260
planning flight routes.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.817
- You can also see Denver,
the aircraft flying west.

00:00:21.817 --> 00:00:22.650
(Patrick speaking
indistinctly on background)

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.270
- [Narrator] So he's here to
explain how that process works

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.093
and how an airlines route
system affects travelers.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.030
- There's two main network structures.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.160
There's hub and spoke, and
there's point to point.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.930
- [Narrator] United and
other legacy airlines,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.360
like Delta and American, use this model.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.500
- The way the hub and spoke works,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.690
you have Denver as a hub
and then you'll have flights

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.020
from, let's say, Lubbock, Albuquerque,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.320
Jackson Hole, and Las Vegas.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.670
These are the spokes;

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.920
the spokes cities all fly into the hub.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.570
The passengers then change airplanes

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.010
and then those flights disperse

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.050
to different cities across the country.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.330
- [Narrator] Here's what that
looks like on a real-time map.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.820
- In Los Angeles and San Francisco,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.980
you can see traffic going out to Hawaii,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.660
traffic intra California,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.784
and then traffic coming up
across the upper northeast.

00:01:07.784 --> 00:01:08.617
(Patrick speaking
indistinctly on background)

00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:09.450
- [Narrator] Here's what that looks like

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.283
on an aircraft schedule.

00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:11.820
- And the color depicts the hub.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.640
Aircraft number six, for
example, it's flying from Korea

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.440
and every time it's in red,

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.150
it's coming back to San Francisco's.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.190
- [Narrator] The spokes are
designed to feed travelers

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.050
into the hub around the same time

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.480
to make connections
easier and more efficient.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.010
But this also creates periods

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:29.130
of very high and very
low volume in the hub.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.030
- You look throughout the day,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.000
and it'll go peak, trough,
peak, trough, peak, trough,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.410
and that drives cost

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.870
because you have a lot of
activity and you have no activity.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.940
- [Narrator] Hub and spoke
is a more expensive model,

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.350
which is why legacy airlines

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.000
are the ones who typically use it.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.540
Low-cost carriers, by definition,
try to keep costs low.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.650
- In order to support the hub,

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:47.820
you need a couple different things.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.520
One, you need the assets,
which is the gates,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.500
the clubs, the lounges,
the terminal space.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.840
The second, you obviously
need the employees.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.010
- [Narrator] Another reason hub and spoke

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.110
is designed this way is to feed travelers

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.690
into larger aircraft
for long-haul flights.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.440
- We'll have aircraft like
this 737 flying into our hub,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.140
connecting to this large 350-seat aircraft

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.890
that's gonna fly to a place
like Frankfurt or Johannesburg.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.050
- [Narrator] Quayle says
that by connecting in a hub,

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:16.050
travelers have more options
when it comes to destinations,

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.200
flight times, and re-booking
in case a flight gets canceled.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.270
- Often, many hub-spoke carriers

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:22.950
have service to multiple hubs.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.680
And so if there's a weather
incident in a major hub,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.840
we could fly you over a different hub.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.100
And then once you get to that city,

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.830
you can be re-accommodated on
the network out of that hub.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.540
- [Narrator] If there's an
issue with the aircraft,

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.240
hubs also typically
have maintenance spaces.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.680
- We can simply tow it to the hangar

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:38.940
and tow in a different aircraft up

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.200
to continue the flight on.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.000
- [Narrator] To pay for
all this infrastructure,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.630
Quayle says legacy carriers
charge a higher ticket price.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.000
- Meaning a better
product, better experience,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.940
and hoping people will
pay more for that product.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.350
- [Narrator] Most low-cost airlines

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.510
like Southwest, Spirit, and Frontier

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.780
use a point-to-point system
which works like this.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.660
- You'd fly from San Antonio to Amarillo.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.690
In Amarillo, that aircraft might
turn and go to Kansas City.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.600
And from Kansas City,
it might go to St. Louis

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.760
and then down to Jacksonville.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.750
- [Narrator] Southwest says

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.370
one benefit of this model is that

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.100
because planes don't have to
connect through a major hub,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.980
the airline can offer more
nonstop flight options.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.240
- If you live in a smaller community,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.010
take San Antonio for example,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.660
there's a low cost airline there

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:25.170
that has 20 different cities
they serve out of San Antonio.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.030
Whereas if you're flying a legacy carrier,

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:27.870
you're gonna need to connect

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.390
in a place like Houston,
Dallas, or Atlanta.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:31.710
- [Narrator] Though
point-to-point carriers

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:34.800
don't have official hubs,
they do have larger locations

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.110
that facilitate many of their connections.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.850
They usually have one fleet type

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.590
which simplifies their operation.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.650
- They only have to have one
crew and one maintenance team

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.020
providing service to
the exact same aircraft.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.190
The downside to this, though,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.040
they're only able to serve
mid-size and large cities,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.960
and they can't fly trans-oceanic.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.670
- [Narrator] Quayle says
one of the challenges

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:54.900
with a point-to-point model

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.300
is the ripple effect if
something goes wrong.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.490
- If you're flying from
San Antonio to Amarillo

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.070
and then from Amarillo to Kansas City,

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:04.410
if the airplane has a problem in Amarillo,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.780
it's gonna then impact the
passengers going to Kansas City

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.730
and those passengers getting
on the plane in Kansas City

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.370
who wanna fly to St.
Louis and then go beyond.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:12.330
- [Narrator] Southwest says

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.280
if a flight is facing a lengthy delay

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.020
or is canceled between cities,

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.030
the airline will bring in another aircraft

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.970
or reroute travelers to
minimize disruptions.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.670
But some industry experts
say the point-to-point model

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.460
may be one reason Southwest
struggled to recover

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.770
from severe winter storms last December

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.430
leading it to cancel over 16,700 flights.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.730
Southwest has held that
the model wasn't to blame.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:37.860
According to Southwest,
a point-to-point model

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.410
brings in more redundancy
during weather challenges

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.360
allowing the airline to get travelers home

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.420
as quickly as possible
through other routings.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.370
All airlines update their route networks

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:48.570
throughout the year.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.280
These are the factors Quayle looks for

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.560
when deciding whether or
not to add a new route.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.450
- What's the nonstop demand?

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.270
So how many people are in the major city

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:58.410
going to the other city?

00:04:58.410 --> 00:04:59.820
How much support is coming in

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.190
from all the different
spokes into that hub

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.800
which provide feed to that new
flight that we'd be adding?

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.410
- [Narrator] If no other airline
flies to that destination,

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.150
that's another reason to add that route.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:10.920
But Quayle says he also adds service

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.546
to places with a lot of competition.

00:05:12.546 --> 00:05:13.379
(airplane whirs)

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:14.460
- In London Heathrow, for example,

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.920
we've increased service from
New York to London Heathrow

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.290
to create more time
channels throughout the day

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.110
for a consumer to go
between New York and London.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.700
- [Narrator] Partnerships
with other airlines

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.190
also play a role in choosing where to fly.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:27.750
- The reason we have as
many flights as we have

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:28.860
into Frankfurt and Munich

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.080
is for their ability to
connect onto Lufthansa

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.420
to go into Eastern
Europe and North Africa.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:34.290
- [Narrator] To add a route,

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.790
Quayle says airlines have to go

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.800
through a government regulatory process

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.180
which prevents other carriers

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.000
from piggybacking off their
idea, and the process can take

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.760
anywhere from a couple
months to a couple years.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.800
- I hope people appreciate the complexity

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.590
and all the planning and work
that goes in behind the scenes

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.180
so that when they show up at the airport,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.550
they can get on a flight to
go from point A to point B

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.040
and have it operate seamlessly.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.990
(light music)

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.150
The funniest misconception
people have about me

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:01.530
when they hear network planning

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.549
is they think that I work in IT.

00:06:03.549 --> 00:06:07.716
(Patrick and camera crew chuckle)

